When one cluster shapes to several glyphs, Ladybird adds letter-spacing
after the cluster’s last glyph only. Blink does the same: ShapeResult::
ApplySpacingOrExpansion() skips each glyph whose character_index is the
same as the next glyph’s. Gecko adds spacing after a character only if
CanAddSpacingAfter() finds that the next character starts both a cluster
and a new ligature group, in nsTextFrame.cpp’s PropertyProvider::
GetSpacingInternal(). And WebKit spaces each glyph alike, cluster or no.

Run in Chrome 157, Firefox 151, and Playwright’s WebKit 26.5 on eleven
samples (Thai, Devanagari, Bengali, Tamil, pointed Hebrew, Vietnamese
stacked diacritics, Khmer, Hangul jamo, Latin, and emoji ZWJ sequences),
Chrome and Firefox put the spacing once per grapheme cluster on every
one, as Ladybird now does. WebKit put it 10 times into 7 Devanagari
clusters and 9 times into 5 Bengali ones, much as Ladybird did. One case
still differs: In a font where HarfBuzz gives the Thai SARA AM a cluster
of its own (Ayuthaya), Chrome and Ladybird space before it, and Firefox,
which counts grapheme clusters instead, doesn’t.
